{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>A talent of gold, etc.</b> In the second chapter of Middot (Menahot 88a) it is taught: ״כִּכַּר זָהָב טָהוֹר״ etc. — we have learned concerning the menorah that it comes from the talent; from where do we include its lamps? Scripture says: ״אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים״. One might think I also include its tongs? Scripture says: ״אֹתָהּ״." "And to grasp the truth of the matter, let me apply my mind to know what was lacking for me in knowledge of the commandment of the menorah, on account of which it was necessary to state this verse. And I see that without this verse I would not have known the amount of gold for the menorah — whether a talent or half of one; therefore the verse came and informed us of the measure: כִּכַּר זָהָב טָהוֹר תַּעֲשֶׂה. And after He informed us of the measure of the menorah, we then entered into a doubt: are the lamps included in the measure or not? And the reason we entered into doubt only about the lamps and not about the tongs and the firepans is that concerning the tongs and firepans the verse explained and said ״זָהָב טָהוֹר״, and if they were from the stated amount of the menorah, it would not have been necessary to say of them ״זָהָב טָהוֹר״; whereas the lamps, regarding which it did not mention gold, remained in doubt whether they are from the talent. And one cannot say that from the fact that it did not say ״זָהָב טָהוֹר״ we may infer that they are from the talent, because it is possible to say that HaShem was not insistent about them that they be either of gold or of other metals, since they serve a function — and in any case they are not from the talent. Therefore the verse says <b>וְאֵת כָּל כֵּלֶיהָ</b> — thus it clarified the doubt we had regarding the lamps and stated that they are from the talent. But tongs and firepans I have no doubt about: from the fact that it was necessary to say of them ״זָהָב טָהוֹר,״ they are certainly not from the talent. And from now on the verse was not concerned to mislead me with the statement ״אֵת כָּל כֵּלֶיהָ,״ into which tongs and firepans also enter." "If so, it is difficult: why did the verse say the word ״אֹתָהּ״ to restrict? If to restrict tongs and firepans — behold, no restriction is needed. And it appears proper to say, by raising a further difficulty in the verse: after the truth that the tongs and firepans are not included in the talent, why did He arrange the commandment of כִּכַּר זָהָב טָהוֹר after He mentioned tongs and firepans — for from this the intellect would be compelled to say that the statement applies to them as well? For if not, He should have first said ״כִּכַּר זָהָב טָהוֹר״ etc. and ״וְאֶת כָּל כֵּלֶיהָ,״ and only afterward HaShem should command ״מַלְקָחֶיהָ וּמַחְתּוֹתֶיהָ,״ and then in any case it would not have been necessary to say the word ״אֹתָהּ״ to restrict her tongs and her firepans, for they are already restricted since they were mentioned only after He said ״כִּכַּר זָהָב״ etc.:" "<b>However,</b> behold, the Master, blessed is He, who gives the Torah, chose to say the verse <b>וּמַלְקָחֶיהָ</b> etc. before saying <b>כִּכָּר זָהָב</b> etc., for two reasons. The first is because He says <b>וּרְאֵה וַעֲשֵׂה בְתַבְנִיתָם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה מָרְאֶה בָּהָר</b> (Exodus 25:40); therefore He juxtaposed this verse to the statement about the menorah and its vessels, which are its lamps, and had He arranged the verse ״כִּכַּר זָהָב״ etc. before ״וּמַלְקָחֶיהָ,״ it would have been understood that the matter applies to them as well, which is not so — for it is possible that HaShem did not show him the form of the tongs and the firepans on the mountain." "And the second reason: to juxtapose the tongs to the lamps — to say that just as the tongs were made on their own, so too the lamps resemble them in this detail, that their making is on their own, even though they are from the talent; it does not follow from this that they must have been hammered out like the calyxes and bowls etc. And although I explained above that from the fact that it did not mention the lamps together with the other details that it mentioned as hammered out, we infer that they are not included — and if so there should have been no need for the aforementioned hint — with all this, since the verse said ״כִּכַּר זָהָב טָהוֹר״ etc. ״אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים״ (Exodus 25:39), behold, it equated them to the menorah, and reason would come to say that in this too the verse intended to include them, that they should be hammered out — for we do not find any detail among the details of the menorah that is from the talent yet is not from the hammering; therefore the verse came and juxtaposed the matter of the tongs and the firepans to the lamps, to say as aforementioned." "<b>Now behold:</b> after there was need in the matter to write the verse ״כִּכַּר זָהָב״ etc. after the verse ״וּמַלְקָחֶיהָ״ etc. for the reasons mentioned, the verse was concerned lest an intelligent person reflect on the matter — for this is indeed His intention, blessed be He — and say: surely He did not speak in this order except to include the tongs and the pans within the weight of the kikkar; and he will strain to find some reason for the verse's saying ״זָהָב טָהוֹר״ as some derivation, as you might say to require it of them, even though regarding the menorah itself the verse used an inclusion of ״תֵּעָשֶׂה הַמְּנוֹרָה״ — in any event, even if made of other metals, these [tongs and pans] would need to be of pure gold. Therefore He, blessed be He, was cunning and said the word <b>אוֹתָהּ</b> to exclude its tongs and its pans, which are not from the kikkar; and He made known the lamps, that they are from the kikkar, by His saying <b>אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים</b>, which stands in its [the menorah's] place. And from now on we may hold that the reason He arranged the verse <b>וּמַלְקָחֶיהָ</b> etc. before the verse <b>כִּכַּר זָהָב</b> etc. is for the reasons we have written; therefore the Tanna derived the word ״אוֹתָהּ״ to exclude its tongs and its pans, and ״אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים״ to include the lamps. And He said <b>כָּל</b> to say that even a single lamp may not be made except from the kikkar. And in this manner I have found room [for explanation] in His saying <b>אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים</b> and not saying ״וְאֵת״ — to say ״אוֹתָהּ אֵת״ etc., ״אֵת כָּל״ etc. ״וְאוֹתָהּ״ — and understand."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {5 "ה׳ in the phrase נִתְחַכֵּם ה׳ refers to HaShem; rendered per the divine-name rule."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:06.627166645Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}